  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 333 AM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nA surface frontal boundary will remain anchored over Kansas \nTuesday night into Wednesday. North of the front, persistent low\nlevel easterly winds will continue into Wednesday. An upper \nlevel trough and associated mid level frontogenesis will \nincrease across central and southern Nebraska Tuesday night into\nWednesday morning, bringing the best chances for precipitation \nfor the forecast area in the long term forecast. The inherited \nforecast has a broad swath of 60 to 70% pops across the central \nand southern portion of the forecast area. Looking at the NBM \nensembles tonight, there is a 70 to 90 percent chance of 12 hr \nQPF> 0.01 inches in these areas ending at 00z Thursday. When \nthis threshold is increased to 0.10 inches, probabilities fall \noff to 50 to 60 percent. The latest deterministic GFS, EC and \nNAM12 solns develop a swath of precipitation across central and \nsouthern Nebraska overnight Tuesday night into Wednesday \nmorning, lending further support to likely pops. Ridging will \nbegin to build northeast from the SW CONUS beginning Thursday \nwith the ridge persisting through Sunday. After readings in the \n70s Wednesday, readings will climb into the 80s Thursday with \n90s returning to the area on Friday. By the weekend some \nreadings up to 100 degrees are possible in the west and \nsouthwest. The latest statistical MEX guidance mentions 102 for \na high Saturday for North Platte and 103 for Valentine. Beyond \nWednesday night, the threat for organized precipitation appears \nlow.",
